<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Alexander I of Russia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1777">1777</a>–<a href="/years/1825">1825</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Alexander I</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Alexander I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> was emperor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> from 1801 to 1825. He led Russia through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleonic-wars">Napoleonic Wars</a>, including the defeat of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s invasion in 1812, and Russian armies reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in 1814. He took a central part in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-vienna">Congress of Vienna</a>, and his early reforming plans gave way to more conservative rule later in his reign.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alexander-i-of-russia.jpg" alt="Alexander I of Russia" width="320" height="408" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: George Dawe · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alexander_I_of_Russia_by_G.Dawe_(1826,_Peterhof)-crop.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Alexander was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-petersburg">Saint Petersburg</a> on 23 December 1777, the eldest son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paul-i-of-russia">Paul I of Russia</a>, and he and his younger brother Constantine were raised by their grandmother, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catherine-the-great">Catherine the Great</a>. He took in the ideas of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-jacques-rousseau">Jean-Jacques Rousseau</a> from the free-thinking atmosphere of her court and from his Swiss tutor, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederic-cesar-de-la-harpe">Frédéric-César de La Harpe</a>, and learned the traditions of Russian autocracy from his military governor, Nikolay Saltykov. He disliked his father as emperor even more than his grandmother, writing that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> had become a plaything for the insane.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Alexander became emperor on 23 March 1801, when his father was assassinated, and was crowned in the Kremlin on 15 September. Historians still debate his role in the murder. The most common theory is that he knew of the conspiracy but insisted that his father should not be killed. He appointed a Private Committee of young friends to plan reforms leading to a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitutional-monarchy">constitutional monarchy</a>. In practice he continued absolutist policies, though he introduced major educational reforms in 1803 and 1804, including more universities. Plans for a parliament and a constitution were never carried out.</p><p>In foreign policy he changed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>'s position towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> four times between 1804 and 1812. After defeats at Austerlitz and Friedland he formed an alliance with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> in the Treaty of Tilsit in 1807, and in 1809 he took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>. The alliance collapsed by 1810, above all over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. Napoleon's invasion of Russia in 1812 was a catastrophe for the French. After the defeat of Napoleon at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leipzig">Leipzig</a> in 1813, Alexander, as supreme commander of the coalition forces in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, ordered them to cross the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>, and allied forces occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in 1814. He formed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-alliance">Holy Alliance</a> to suppress revolutionary movements in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p><p>From the end of 1818 his liberal views began to change, and Metternich gained influence over him. In the second half of his reign he became increasingly arbitrary and fearful of plots, ended many earlier reforms and purged schools of foreign teachers. Speransky was replaced as adviser by Aleksey Arakcheyev, who oversaw the creation of military settlements.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In the autumn of 1825 Alexander travelled to the south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> because his wife was ill. He caught <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/typhus">typhus</a> and died at Taganrog on 19 November 1825 by the Old Style calendar. He left no legitimate children, since his two daughters had died in childhood, and neither of his brothers wished to become emperor. After a period of confusion his younger brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicholas-i-of-russia">Nicholas I of Russia</a> succeeded him. A popular legend holds that he faked his death and lived as a hermit under the name Feodor Kuzmich. Historians regard him as a complex and often contradictory ruler whose achievements in foreign policy were not matched by equally far-reaching domestic reforms.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Alexander I of Russia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After his defeat in Russia in 1812, Prussia insisted that the duchy be eliminated, while Tsar <b>Alexander I</b> wished to annex it; Austria and the United Kingdom, through Lord Castlereagh, strongly opposed him.</blockquote><a href="/empire/congress-poland">Congress Poland · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The central apparatus changed over the imperial period: Peter I's collegiate system of administrative boards (1718) gave way under <b>Alexander I</b> to eight ministries (1802), which divided executive authority more rationally by function.</blockquote><a href="/empire/russian-empire">Russian Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Many officers were at first encouraged by the early liberal reforms of Tsar <b>Alexander I</b>, when liberalism was officially encouraged and expectations ran high.</blockquote><a href="/movement/decembrist-revolt">Decembrist revolt · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>Three emperors ruled in these decades: <b>Alexander I</b> (1801–1825), who drove Napoleon’s army out of Russia in 1812; his brother Nicholas I (1825–1855), who began his reign by crushing the Decembrist revolt of noble officers; and Nicholas’s son Alexander II (1855–1881), who freed the serfs.</blockquote><a href="/societies/russian-empire">Social Hierarchy of the Russian Empire</a></li><li><blockquote>The Rainers were singing it around Christmas 1819, once performed it before an audience that included Franz I of Austria and <b>Alexander I of Russia</b>, and gave its first performance in the United States, in New York City in 1839.</blockquote><a href="/songs/silent-night">Silent Night</a></li><li><blockquote>Napoleon and Emperor <b>Alexander I of Russia</b> signed the first of the Treaties of Tilsit, ending hostilities between France and Russia in the War of the Fourth Coalition after the French victory at Friedland.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-7">7 July in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-petersburg">Saint Petersburg</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. Died in Taganrog, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=59.95,30.32&amp;year=1808" aria-label="Saint Petersburg on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5185 154 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Alexander I of Russia"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5635 424h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5635 424h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=59.95,30.32&amp;year=1808">What was here in 1808? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
